Standalone Neon heap WAL handlers
================================

This fork unconditionally emits RM_NEON_ID (134) heap WAL. Without the
matching redo and logical decoding functions, a standalone database cannot
recover its own acknowledged writes after a crash or stream them to replicas.

These handlers derive from pgxn/neon_rmgr in neondatabase/neon commit
fa504217c61bbcaf5c512d75830564541f917f8f. See LICENSE and NOTICE. They require
no pageserver, safekeeper, commercial platform or separately installed module.

The fallback is installed after shared_preload_libraries have been loaded.
An explicitly preloaded extension that registers rmgr 134 takes precedence.
The registration does not change WAL record IDs or layouts, or the storage
manager selection. Resource manager 134 remains in the custom ID range.

Adaptations from the PG17 implementation:
* Compile the handlers into the backend and register only an absent rmgr 134.
* Use PostgreSQL 18's ReorderBufferAllocChange/AllocTupleBuf APIs.
* Calculate insert payload sizes using SizeOfNeonHeapHeader, and subtract
  SizeOfNeonHeapDelete (rather than a tuple header) before decoding old rows.
* Reuse the identical core heap_mask implementation for consistency checks.
* Retain the base snapshot during PG18 fast-forward decoding so catalog_xmin
  protects catalog tuples required by later logical decoding.
* Remove unused PG16 branches and extension module/bootstrap wrappers.
* Use distinct exported symbol names so separately preloaded neon_rmgr
  libraries bind their decoding callbacks to their own implementations.

The complete native recovery, subscription, logical decoding, WAL consistency
and SQL tests are required for this code. A single crash-recovery smoke is
not sufficient to establish correctness for all record types. The development
qualification harness also preserves failures from the original handler-less
source. See docs/qualification/ for the measured validation state.
